  "query_preview": "..\n\nKey Message 1...Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms continue \nthrough this evening with strong winds as the main hazard. \n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms early this afternoon have spread \nacross the Lake Ontario region on both sides of the international \nborder associated with a compact shortwave noted on GOES WV imagery \nlifting just north of Toronto. This is all while earlier storms \nacross the Southern Tier outran the axis of strongest instability \nleading to a swath of stratiform precipitation lingering. \nRegardless, severe thunderstorms were still observed this morning \nwith gusty winds leading to downed trees and small hail reported. \nClearing skies moving into western NY early this afternoon per GOES \nsatellite imagery will help potentially refuel the atmosphere for \nthe next round of strong to severe thunderstorms. \n\nOngoing development in eastern MI and southwestern ON within an area \nof 2000 J/kg of MLCAPE and 40 kt of bulk shear closer to the cold \nfront will be worth monitoring as it moves east towards western NY \nlater this afternoon into early this evening. Additionally, \nsouthwestward expansion of the ongoing convection near Toronto along \nthe prefrontal trough associated with the aforementioned \nshortwave will also need close monitoring as atmospheric \ndestabilization continues. These storms will progress eastward \nacross western and north-central NY through the afternoon and \nevening.\n\nStrong winds and heavy rainfall will continue to be the primary \nhazard with these storms. PWAT value reported from the KBUF 12z \nsounding was 1.50\" and warm cloud depths exceed 10 kft. However, \nfast storm motions and line normal motions, as observed with earlier \nstorms, would limit heavy rainfall durations. Additionally, the \nhighest DCAPE values remain in southwestern ON and will be less than \nwhat was analyzed earlier across the Niagara Frontier around the \ntime of severe wind damage reported.  \n\nHeaded into late this evening, lingering showers and a few rumbles \nof thunder may continue until the cold front passes through western \nNY around midnight and early Wednesday morning for north-central NY. \n\nKey Message 2...Cool Wednesday, with a chance of lingering \nshowers ahead of a gradual warm up through the remainder of the \nweek.\n\nCooler weather is expected Wednesday as the upper level low begins \nto exit to the northeast. With 850mb temps dropping around 6-8 degC, \nafternoon highs will struggle to reach 70 degF as most locations \nwill be around 10 degF below normal for late July. Additionally, a \nsecondary shortwave will round the backside of the departing low, \nsupporting scattered showers along with the cooler air aloft and \nsteep low-level lapse rates extending inland from the lakes under \nwest-northwest flow. High pressure will begin to build into the \nlower Great Lakes late Wednesday through the remainder of the week \nleading to drier weather and gradually warming temperatures \nback towards slightly above normal this weekend.",
